---
import GameStudy from '../../layouts/GameStudy.astro';
import { isFeatureEnabled } from '../../config/features';
import { Person } from '../../components/design/SaasGameUI/WorldArtwork';
import { Creature } from '../../components/design/SaasGameUI/CreatureArt';
if (!isFeatureEnabled('enableSaasGameUI')) return Astro.redirect('/portfolio');
---
<GameStudy title="SaaS Game UI — a small design system" description="A rudimentary design system for operating software through game interactions. Three playable studies explore AI harnesses, scheduled workers, and agent coordination.">
  <main class="ds-page">
    <nav class="ds-nav" aria-label="Design study"><a href="/portfolio">← shupp.dev</a><span>Design studies / SaaS Game UI</span><a href="https://github.com/shuppel/shupp-dev/pull/55">MR 55 ↗</a></nav>
    <header class="ds-hero"><div><span class="g-eyebrow">SaaS Game UI · Rudimentary system / 01</span><h1>Make the interface<br/>a place.</h1></div><p>A character can be an agent. Equipment can be a capability. A creature can be your personal assistant. Borrow the clarity of a 2D JRPG to make software’s actions, dependencies, and consequences visible.</p></header>
    <section aria-labelledby="play-studies"><div class="ds-section-label" id="play-studies">01 / Play the idea</div><div class="ds-games">
      <a class="ds-game" href="/design/saas-game-ui/fieldwork"><div class="ds-game-art"><div class="ds-mini-person"><Person kind="operator"/></div><span class="ds-mini-object" aria-hidden="true">▤</span></div><div class="ds-game-copy"><span class="g-eyebrow">01 · Embodied interaction</span><h2>Fieldwork</h2><p>Walk, collect context, equip your party, and recover a patch. Operate an AI harness through a character.</p><strong>Enter the field <span>↗</span></strong></div></a>
      <a class="ds-game" href="/design/saas-game-ui/creature-works"><div class="ds-game-art ds-art-creature"><Creature kind="finch"/><Creature kind="sprout" equipment={['lens','scarf']} level={3}/><Creature kind="moth"/></div><div class="ds-game-copy"><span class="g-eyebrow">02 · Companion progression</span><h2>Creature Works</h2><p>Adopt a little assistant. Give it missions, review its results, earn XP, and equip a new specialty.</p><strong>Meet your companion <span>↗</span></strong></div></a>
      <a class="ds-game" href="/design/saas-game-ui/relay-guild"><div class="ds-game-art ds-art-relay"><div class="ds-mini-person"><Person kind="operator"/></div><div class="ds-mini-person"><Person kind="implementer"/></div><div class="ds-mini-person"><Person kind="verifier"/></div></div><div class="ds-game-copy"><span class="g-eyebrow">03 · Agent coordination</span><h2>Relay Guild</h2><p>Queue party orders. Pass evidence between agents. Watch waiting work resume when its input arrives.</p><strong>Gather the party <span>↗</span></strong></div></a>
    </div></section>
    <section class="ds-companion-pattern" id="companions" aria-labelledby="companion-pattern-title"><div class="ds-companion-example"><Creature kind="sprout" equipment={['lens','planner','scarf']} level={3}/><span>Lv. 3 · Trusted helper</span></div><div><span class="g-eyebrow">Pattern / Companionship</span><h2 id="companion-pattern-title">An assistant you grow with.</h2><p>A name and a personality build attachment. Missions give it purpose. Accepted results earn XP and equipment; that equipment opens a specialty. The journal remembers what you accomplished together.</p><div class="ds-companion-loop"><span>Adopt</span><i>→</i><span>Equip</span><i>→</i><span>Mission</span><i>→</i><span>Review</span><i>→</i><span>Grow</span></div><a href="/design/saas-game-ui/creature-works">Try the companion loop ↗</a></div></section>
    <section class="ds-chapter" id="language" aria-labelledby="language-title"><div class="ds-section-label">02 / The shared language</div><div class="ds-chapter-intro"><h2 id="language-title">Every game action needs a software consequence.</h2><p>The world is the working surface. A focused command panel exposes the next action; inventory, settings, and evidence appear when requested. Six primitives connect the scene to the application.</p></div><div class="ds-mapping">
      <article class="ds-primitive"><div class="ds-specimen"><div class="ds-mini-person"><Person kind="implementer"/></div><Creature kind="sprout"/></div><h3>Actor</h3><span>Agent / worker / operator</span><p>A named entity with a job, capabilities, and current state. Select its character to inspect and direct it.</p></article>
      <article class="ds-primitive"><div class="ds-specimen"><code>search.ts</code><em>→ context</em></div><h3>World object</h3><span>Input / destination / output</span><p>A meaningful resource you can approach, target, collect, or inspect. Objects retain a useful identity.</p></article>
      <article class="ds-primitive"><div class="ds-specimen"><span class="ds-command">▸ Research → Archive</span></div><h3>Command</h3><span>Action + target</span><p>Choose an actor, choose an action, then its target. Preview the consequence before execution.</p></article>
      <article class="ds-primitive"><div class="ds-specimen"><Creature kind="moth" upgrades={['wings','retry']}/></div><h3>Equipment</h3><span>Context / tool / policy</span><p>Changes what an actor can do or how it runs. Wings change duration. A charm changes retry behavior.</p></article>
      <article class="ds-primitive"><div class="ds-specimen"><span class="ds-status">✓ 4 checks passed</span></div><h3>Feedback</h3><span>State / progress / result</span><p>Movement, labels, and marks reveal what is happening. Completion follows verified work.</p></article>
      <article class="ds-primitive"><div class="ds-specimen"><div class="ds-log-lines"><span>00:05 · Scheduled run</span><span>00:08 · Digest delivered</span><span>00:10 · Next occurrence</span></div></div><h3>Record</h3><span>Stats / log / evidence</span><p>Open the character’s dashboard for exact values, history, and artifacts. The metaphor has an inspectable truth.</p></article>
    </div></section>
    <section class="ds-chapter" aria-labelledby="foundations-title"><div class="ds-section-label">03 / Visual foundations</div><div class="ds-chapter-intro"><h2 id="foundations-title">A quiet frame.<br/>A legible little world.</h2><p>Smooth 2D characters, a readable field, and restrained JRPG panels. Use modern type and familiar controls wherever precision matters.</p></div><div class="ds-foundations">
      <div class="ds-foundation"><h3>Color has a job</h3><div class="ds-swatches" aria-label="Midnight, panel blue, parchment, gold, sage"><span style="background:#101d29"></span><span style="background:#192d3e"></span><span style="background:#eee7d6"></span><span style="background:#dec18a"></span><span style="background:#90aa7f"></span></div><p>Midnight canvas · blue panels · parchment text.<br/>Gold identifies selection and intent. Sage supports completed work. Status always has a text label.</p></div>
      <div class="ds-foundation"><h3>Inter, throughout</h3><div class="ds-type">Aa 0123</div><p>One family. Regular body, medium labels.<br/>10–12px metadata · 14–16px content · 26–36px game titles. Large editorial type belongs outside the game.</p></div>
      <div class="ds-foundation"><h3>Frames, with purpose</h3><div class="ds-frame-sample"><span>Actor → command → consequence</span></div><p>Thin borders group related controls. A second inset line adds the JRPG reference. Use a 4px spacing base and clear separation between field and commands.</p></div>
    </div></section>
    <section class="ds-chapter" aria-labelledby="rules-title"><div class="ds-section-label">04 / Interaction principles</div><div class="ds-chapter-intro"><h2 id="rules-title">Keep the play.<br/>Respect the work.</h2><p>The system is useful when the game makes a relationship easier to understand. These are the rules each study exercises.</p></div><ol class="ds-rules">
      <li><b>Make selection distinct from execution.</b>Approaching an object or selecting an actor opens its available actions. Targeted commands have a confirmation step.</li>
      <li><b>Reveal one layer at a time.</b>The field shows who and where. Selection shows what can happen. A dashboard or log explains the exact state.</li>
      <li><b>Make upgrades change behavior.</b>Equipment controls context, tools, duration, and retries. The creature’s appearance reflects its configuration.</li>
      <li><b>Show dependencies in the world.</b>An agent can wait for an artifact. A handoff transfers that input and lets the queued work continue.</li>
      <li><b>Keep the controls accessible.</b>Keyboard and touch have equivalent commands. Reduced motion removes travel animation. Native inputs handle exact settings.</li>
      <li><b>Leave the player an exit.</b>Escape cancels active targeting first, then opens the game menu. Resume the scene or return here to understand its design.</li>
    </ol></section>
    <aside class="ds-boundary"><h2>A working design study.<br/>A clear simulation boundary.</h2><p>All three games operate local application state. Agent text and job outputs are authored examples. Checks execute in the browser. Creature Works uses sample mission data, an earned game currency, and local saves. Its recurring jobs use a simulated UTC clock. Fieldwork and Relay Guild reset when their pages are left. Connecting a real agent or scheduler is a separate integration.</p></aside>
    <footer class="ds-footer"><span>Three playable studies · One evolving language</span><a href="#play-studies">Choose a game ↑</a></footer>
  </main>
</GameStudy>
